\ ======================================================================
\ 09 — a type that cannot be copied, and one that can.
\
\ THE CLAIM (D-08, D-94, D-95): linearity is not a mode, an annotation or a
\ borrow checker. It is a CAPABILITY LIST on a declaration. `M01.has_cap`
\ reads a sealed type's capabilities off the type and never looks at what it
\ wraps, so a linear handle over a perfectly copyable `i64` is expressible —
\ and `dup` at that type simply has no signature.
\
\ The other direction is the one that has to be checked, and D-94 is where:
\ sealing may DROP a capability and may never ADD one.
\ ======================================================================

\ --- 1. A linear type. No `cap` clause, so it has neither capability.

seal Fd ( i64 ) { pack fd unpack fd_raw }

3 fd

\ `<0:3>` — nominal id 0, holding 3. The value prints its identity because
\ that is what it has; there is no name in a `dtype` to recover.

\ --- 2. `dup` and `pop` are capability-gated in the ELABORATOR, before M06
\ ever sees the term, so the message names the value and not the primitive.

dup

pop

\ It has to be consumed explicitly, which is the whole point of linearity.

fd_raw show print "\n" print

\ --- 3. The same representation, declared `copy` and `drop`.

seal Meters ( i64 ) { cap copy cap drop pack meters unpack raw_m }

7 meters dup raw_m show print " " print raw_m show print "\n" print

\ Two `i64`s underneath, one type that duplicates and one that does not.
\ `M01.lemma_seal_caps` is the formal version: a seal's capabilities are
\ independent of its representation.

\ --- 4. SEALING NARROWS AND NEVER WIDENS (D-94). Independence runs one way.
\ `Box` is a unique owning pointer and has neither capability, so a wrapper
\ claiming `copy` over one would alias it the moment `unpack` handed it back.

seal Cheat ( Box[i64] ) { cap copy pack cheat }

\ `M06.prim_sig` refuses `PPack` there too, so the rule holds for any term and
\ not merely for ones this elaborator produced.

\ --- 5. SEALS ARE NOMINAL, and this is what `data` cannot do. Two seals over
\ the same representation are two types.

seal Gd ( i64 ) { pack gd unpack gd_raw }

3 gd fd_raw

3 fd fd_raw show print "\n" print

\ Compare demo 08 §10: two `data` declarations with the same variant shapes
\ ARE interchangeable, because `TSum` has nowhere to put an identity.
\ `TSeal` carries a `nom_id`, and the session allocates a fresh one per
\ declaration — so the id in `<0:3>` above is the whole of the difference.

\ --- 6. `locate` on a constructor shows the declaration and the operation.

locate fd

locate gd_raw

\ --- 7. Type parameters, and where inference stops. `pack` infers, because
\ what it consumes is on the stack.

seal Cell[#T] ( #T ) { cap copy cap drop pack cell unpack uncell }

5 cell

"hi" cell

\ `unpack` runs the OTHER way: its input is the sealed type, so nothing it
\ consumes can bind #T. It is written out, which is D-93's rule again.

uncell

uncell[str] print "\n" print

uncell[i64] show print "\n" print

\ --- 8. A parameterised seal's capability claim is checked at every
\ INSTANTIATION, because `cap copy` over `( #T )` is a claim about #T.

define hold ( Cell[Box[i64]] -- ) { }

\ --- 9. WHAT IS NOT HERE. There are no modules, so the only control over who
\ may open a seal is whether the declaration names an unpack word at all.

seal Token ( i64 ) { pack token }

1 token

\ Nothing can open it and nothing can discard it, so it is stuck on the stack
\ for the rest of the session — an abstract, linear, unforgeable value, and a
\ demonstration that the guarantee is against accident and not against a
\ caller who can write the unpack name.
